<%# locals: current: -- the current component name (String, e.g. "table"),
    TablerUi::Docs::Navigation::FORM_BUILDER ("form_builder"), or nil on a
    page with no single current entry (the index). Rendered on every docs
    page (see docs/app/views/layouts/tabler_ui/docs/application.html.erb),
    so the full component tree is always one click away.

    Grouped exactly like TablerUi::Docs::Navigation::CATEGORIES, plus a
    "Forms" pane underneath for form_builder's own top-level entry -- see
    navigation.rb's comment on FORM_BUILDER for why it has no category of
    its own (it isn't an app/components/tabler_ui directory, so
    Navigation.components/CATEGORIES correctly omit it). The html: /
    <part>_html: reference page (Navigation::HTML_ATTRIBUTES) and the
    design editor (Navigation::EDITOR) are the same story, and sit above
    the categories instead, alongside "Overview" -- both are cross-cutting
    pages, not a component or a sibling concern like form_builder.

    Each category, and Forms, is a Bootstrap collapse pane rendered by
    shared/_sidebar_category, so both call sites stay byte-identical --
    data-bs-toggle="collapse" / data-controller="tabler-ui--collapse" on the
    pane itself, the exact same machinery
    app/components/tabler_ui/accordion/_component.html.erb drives (checked
    before writing this), reused rather than reinvented per rule 6. Only
    the pane `current` belongs to starts open. The trigger's own "collapsed"
    class is set server-side to match that starting state (mirrors
    Accordion::Component#button_classes) so the chevron renders in the
    right rotation before Bootstrap's JS has run at all -- Bootstrap's
    Collapse then keeps that class in sync on every toggle afterwards, same
    as it does for the trigger array of any other collapse it manages.

    No "Design editor" entry up top any more -- this sidebar never renders
    on the editor page at all now (see application.html.erb's own comment
    on `wide_layout`), and the top navbar carries a link to it on every
    docs page instead (see the layout's "Documentation"/"Editor" nav items),
    so a second link to the same page from inside this sidebar would be
    pure duplication rather than a second, distinct route to it. %>
<nav class="docs-sidebar" aria-label="Components">
  <div class="list-group list-group-transparent mb-3">
    <%= link_to "Overview", root_path,
                 class: ["list-group-item", "list-group-item-action", ("active" if current.nil?)].compact.join(" ") %>
    <%= link_to "HTML attributes", html_attributes_path,
                 class: ["list-group-item", "list-group-item-action",
                         ("active" if current == TablerUi::Docs::Navigation::HTML_ATTRIBUTES)].compact.join(" ") %>
  </div>

  <% TablerUi::Docs::Navigation.category_names.each do |category| %>
    <% category_components = TablerUi::Docs::Navigation.components_in(category) %>
    <% entries = category_components.map { |name| { text: TablerUi::Docs::Navigation.title_for(name), href: component_path(name), active: current == name } } %>
    <%= render "tabler_ui/docs/shared/sidebar_category",
                label: category, id_slug: category.parameterize,
                open: category_components.include?(current), entries: entries %>
  <% end %>

  <%= render "tabler_ui/docs/shared/sidebar_category",
              label: "Forms", id_slug: "forms",
              open: current == TablerUi::Docs::Navigation::FORM_BUILDER,
              entries: [{ text: "Form builder", href: forms_path,
                          active: current == TablerUi::Docs::Navigation::FORM_BUILDER }] %>
</nav>
